# Byte-level freezing is the point of harness/presets/: a preset that drifts
# measures a different experiment. Git must never rewrite these bytes, so they
# are marked non-text and the recorded hash is checked against the raw file.
harness/presets/*.yaml -text -diff
harness/presets/*.sha256 -text -diff

# Same reason, for the delivered form of the same track. These are the bytes a
# run actually loads and the bytes packages/chtd/index.mjs checks its hash
# against, so a checkout that rewrote line endings would refuse to load.
packages/chtd/presets/*.mjs -text -diff
packages/chtd/presets/*.sha256 -text -diff

# The captured harness prefix is a record of bytes a live run sent, and the
# project treats it as one: `docs/finetune.md` points a reader at it to check what
# the harness disclosed. A checkout that rewrote its line endings would hand that
# reader different bytes from the ones recorded. (Its semantics survive either
# way — it is JSON — so this is about the record staying the record, not about
# the file refusing to parse.)
harness/prefix/*.json -text -diff

# A collection profile's *bytes* decide the track. Measured 2026-10-12: the
# harness records a `resolvedProfileHash` over the profile it loaded, and a track
# digest is sha256 over that hash and the preset. Changing one URL — or only the
# line endings — changes the hash, changes the track, and makes a dataset built
# under the old profile refuse to mix with the new one (distill/dataset.py).
# A checkout that rewrote line endings would therefore silently move every
# collection onto its own private track, so these files are frozen like the
# presets are.
harness/profile/*.yaml -text -diff
